        ["Why is the adult rule of nines inaccurate for young children?",["Children's heads make up a larger proportion of body surface area","Children's legs make up a larger proportion of body surface area","Children's skin is thicker, so burns are shallower","Children's trunk accounts for less than 10% of body surface area"],"Infants and young children have relatively larger heads and smaller legs, so pediatric charts such as Lund-Browder are used."],
        ["A patient has burns to the anterior neck. Which position BEST prevents contracture?",["Neck in extension","Neck in flexion with a pillow behind the head","Neck rotated fully to one side","Neck flexed laterally toward the burn"],"Anterior neck burns contract into flexion. Positioning in extension (e.g., no pillow, a towel roll under the shoulders) counters it."],
        ["A patient has burns to both axillae. Which position is MOST appropriate to prevent contracture?",["Shoulder abduction to about 90°","Shoulder adduction with the arms at the sides","Shoulder internal rotation across the chest","Shoulder extension with the elbows flexed"],"Axillary burns contract into adduction. Positioning in abduction (about 90°) maintains range."],
        ["A patient has a dorsal hand burn. In which position should the hand be splinted to prevent contracture?",["Wrist extended, MCPs flexed, IPs extended, thumb abducted","Wrist flexed, MCPs extended, IPs flexed, thumb adducted","Wrist neutral, all finger joints fully flexed","Wrist extended, MCPs extended, IPs flexed"],"The intrinsic-plus (safe) position keeps the MCP collateral ligaments and IP volar structures on stretch to prevent the claw deformity."],
        ["A patient with a burn says that lying with the knees bent on a pillow is most comfortable. What is the MOST appropriate response?",["Position the knees in extension and explain contracture risk","Allow the position because comfort reduces pain medication needs","Add a second pillow to increase knee flexion further","Allow the position at night and extend the knees only during therapy"],"The position of comfort (flexion) is the position of contracture. Positioning against it is essential."],
        ["A raised burn scar is thick and red but stays within the borders of the original burn. What is this scar MOST likely to be?",["Hypertrophic scar","Keloid scar","Atrophic scar","Normal mature scar"],"Hypertrophic scars stay within the wound borders. Keloids extend beyond them."],
        ["A firefighter has a full-thickness circumferential burn of the forearm. Over several hours, his fingers become cool and pale, and capillary refill is delayed. Which intervention is MOST likely needed?",["Escharotomy","Pressure garment fitting","Elevation with ice","Serial casting"],"Rigid circumferential eschar plus edema can compress vessels like a tourniquet. Escharotomy releases the constriction to restore circulation."],
        ["A patient rescued from a house fire has soot around the mouth, singed nasal hair, and a hoarse voice. What is the PRIMARY concern?",["Inhalation injury threatening the airway","Later hypertrophic scarring of the face","Flexion contracture of the anterior neck","Wound infection of the facial burns"],"These are classic signs of inhalation injury, which can cause airway edema and obstruction within hours and is a medical priority."],
        ["Why might a burn that looked superficial partial-thickness on day 1 appear deeper by day 3?",["The zone of stasis can progress to necrosis","The zone of hyperemia always becomes necrotic","Blisters always convert burns to full thickness","Dressings convert partial burns to deeper ones"],"Tissue in the zone of stasis has marginal perfusion and may die if edema, infection, or poor resuscitation intervenes, so burns are reassessed over several days."],
        ["A patient has a scattered burn pattern on the trunk. Which method gives a reasonable quick estimate of the TBSA of these small areas?",["The patient's palm with fingers is about 1%","The patient's fist is about 5% of TBSA","Each scattered area counts as 9% of TBSA","The patient's foot is about 9% of TBSA"],"The palm including the fingers is roughly 1% TBSA and is useful for scattered or irregular burns."],
        ["Three days after a split-thickness skin graft to the posterior knee, the surgeon has ordered immobilization. What is the MOST appropriate PT action?",["Maintain immobilization and avoid shear over the graft","Begin aggressive knee flexion ROM to prevent contracture","Perform scar massage over the graft to improve adherence","Apply a pressure garment directly over the new graft"],"New grafts need a short period of immobilization to adhere and revascularize. ROM resumes after the ordered period, and scar massage and garments come later."]
